    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A group of walled city-kingdoms in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, Katsina, Zazzau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daura">Daura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>.</li><li>They never united, but shared a language, court institutions and a trade network linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the forest.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> (1804–1808) replaced their dynasties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The fine goatskin leather sold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> leather’ was largely tanned and dyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> craftsmen and carried across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

