    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zaria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zaria.jpg" alt="Zaria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zazzau_Emirs_Palace_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aminucrus, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms grew out of farming communities on the savanna of what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>. Around the end of the first millennium some of these communities built fortified settlements, called <em>birni</em>, around sacred hills, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">iron-working</a> sites and markets, and their leaders took the title <em>sarki</em>. Hausa tradition explains their origin through the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayajidda">Bayajidda</a>, a prince from the east who killed a sacred snake at the well of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daura">Daura</a> and married its queen; their descendants were said to have founded the seven ‘legitimate’ states, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa-bakwai">Hausa Bakwai</a> of Daura, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, Katsina, Zazzau (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>, Rano and Biram, while seven other states, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banza-bakwai">Banza Bakwai</a>, including Zamfara, Kebbi, Yauri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a>, were considered less legitimate offshoots. The legend is a charter rather than a record, and historians see the states as forming gradually between roughly the tenth and fourteenth centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a>, written down in the nineteenth century from older traditions, names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagauda">Bagauda</a> as the first king of Kano, traditionally around 999. Kano and Katsina emerged as the strongest commercial centres, Zazzau expanded southwards as a raiding frontier state, and Gobir in the north guarded the approaches from the desert. They never formed a single empire, but shared one language, similar institutions and an expanding network of trade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bilma"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bilma.jpg" alt="Bilma" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bilma">Bilma</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erg_of_bilma.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">T L Miles, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The economy of Hausaland rested on intensive farming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, worked largely by household groups called <em>gandu</em> and, increasingly, by slaves on estates around the towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> cities became the leading manufacturing centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> was famous for cotton cloth dyed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> pits, and Hausa tanners produced fine goatskin leather that reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> as ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> leather’. Merchants carried cloth, leather and grain north across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> through Agadez, imported salt from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilma">Bilma</a> and horses from the north, and traded southwards for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a> from the forest zone of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">Cowrie shells</a> became the common currency in later centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was introduced through traders: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Wangarawa</a> merchants from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> with bringing it to Kano under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-yaji">Sarki Yaji</a> in the fourteenth century. For centuries Islam was mainly the religion of courts and merchants, practised alongside the older <em>bori</em> spirit cults and the veneration of local deities, and many rulers combined both. Muslim scholars, Arabic learning and the writing of Hausa in Arabic script, known as <em>ajami</em>, spread through the towns. By the eighteenth century Hausa had become the main trade language across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/songhai-empire.jpg" alt="Songhai Empire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Songhay_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HetmanTheResearcher, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Warfare between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa states</a> was frequent, and military power rested on armoured cavalry. Wealthy horsemen wore quilted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> armour or imported chain mail and fought with lances and swords, supported by infantry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> using poisoned arrows. Every capital was protected by massive earthen walls with fortified gates, enclosing farmland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> so that the population could withstand sieges; the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, extended under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rumfa">Muhammad Rumfa</a> in the fifteenth century, ran for many kilometres. Kano and Katsina fought a long series of wars over trade and territory, including a protracted conflict in Rumfa’s time. The states were also exposed to stronger neighbours. Kano paid tribute to the Kanem-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a> from about the mid-fifteenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a> probably campaigned in Hausaland around 1513, although the extent of Songhai control is debated. Soon afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-kanta">Kanta Kotal</a> of Kebbi threw off Songhai rule and for much of the sixteenth century made Kebbi the leading power of the western Hausa region. In the seventeenth century the Kwararafa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jukun-people">Jukun</a>) confederacy from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue</a> valley raided Kano and Katsina. In the eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> rose to military prominence, destroying the capital of Zamfara around 1762 and fighting Katsina and Kano, wars that exhausted the region before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emir.jpg" alt="Emir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuja_Shah_Durrani_and_Men_in_his_decorated_palace_Wellcome_V0050531.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdom was governed by a sarki chosen from eligible royal lineages by a council of senior officials, and his authority combined sacred and political roles. Courts developed elaborate hierarchies of titled offices, such as the <em>galadima</em>, who acted as chief minister, the <em>madawaki</em>, who commanded the cavalry, and officers responsible for the treasury, markets and the palace. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> a council of nine senior office-holders advised the ruler, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rumfa">Muhammad Rumfa</a> (r. c. 1463–1499) strengthened royal power by building the palace still used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of Kano, founding the Kurmi market, appointing slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> to high office and introducing Islamic reforms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghrebi</a> scholar Muhammad al-Maghili visited Kano in his reign and wrote for him a treatise on the obligations of Muslim rulers. Revenue came from market dues, taxes on crafts and caravans, tribute from subject villages and the proceeds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a>. Territorial officials collected these through networks of village heads. Justice combined customary law with Islamic law administered by <em>alkali</em> judges in the larger towns. Because succession was contested among royal lineages, and because ruling houses depended on the support of titled officials and wealthy merchants, Hausa monarchies were frequently unstable, and Muslim scholars increasingly criticised rulers for corruption and heavy taxation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/niger-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niger-river.jpg" alt="Niger River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niger,_Niamey,_Pont_Kennedy_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NigerTZai, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms created the commercial and urban core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a>, and their influence outlasted their dynasties. Their towns became the leading markets between the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>, linking the trans-Saharan trade with the forest zone, and Hausa merchants established communities, called <em>zongo</em> in the south, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. Through them the Hausa language spread as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>, and it is today spoken by tens of millions of people as a first or second language, making it one of the most widely spoken languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The court titles, tax systems, walled cities and markets of the Hausa states were largely taken over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled through the institutions they had overthrown, and many of them survive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, Katsina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaria">Zaria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> is among the most important indigenous historical sources for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. Hausa cloth, leatherwork and architecture, including the decorated mud palaces and mosques of Kano and Zaria, remain distinctive. The criticisms that Muslim reformers made of Hausa rulers also shaped the ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> that ended them, which became a model for later reform movements across West Africa.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms were weakened in the late eighteenth century by continual warfare, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>’s campaigns against Zamfara, Katsina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, by heavy taxation and by tensions between rulers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> pastoralists and Muslim scholars. In Gobir the preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> built a large following that demanded strict application of Islamic law, the abolition of non-Islamic taxes and an end to the enslavement of Muslims. When Sarki <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a> of Gobir moved against his community, Usman withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudu">Gudu</a> in February 1804 and was proclaimed commander of the faithful. His followers defeated a Gobir army at Tabkin Kwotto in June 1804, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> spread as local Fulani leaders received flags from Usman to raise revolt in other kingdoms. Katsina, Kano, Zazzau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daura">Daura</a> fell to the jihadists between about 1805 and 1808, and in 1808 the Gobir capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a>, was taken and Yunfa killed. Fulani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> replaced the Hausa dynasties within the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. Some royal lines continued in independent successor states: Zazzau’s rulers founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleja">Abuja</a>, Katsina’s rulers settled at Maradi, and Gobir’s at Tsibiri, from where they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> for decades. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> themselves passed under British rule in 1903.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms built the walled market cities, crafts and trade networks that made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a> one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>’s most productive regions, spread the Hausa language far beyond their borders, and left institutions that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> and the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> inherited.</em></p>

